/// @brief Enum of all time units known to the time Core module
enum TimeUnit:
	NS, // Nanoseconds
	US, // Microseconds
	MS, // Milliseconds
	S;  // Seconds

/// @brief A single time stamp measuring the uptime of the system
///
/// @note A TimeStamp *always* uses the highest resolution available:
///       - On Windows, this is ~10 MHz (100 ns) but do *not* assume that TimeStamps are always 100%
///         precise. Flint uses `QueryPerformanceCounter` under the hood on Windows.
///         https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/sysinfo/acquiring-high-resolution-time-stamps
///       - On Linux, `clock_gettime` with a monotonic clock is used under the hood for time stamp
///         readouts. Depending on harware, you could get precision of up to 1 ns on Linux but do,
///         again, not assume any precision here whatsoever.
///         Linux 7.2 dropped support for CPUs which do not support TSC, so if you are on Linux 7.2
///         or higher you can assume a precision of ~1 ns
/// @note As a result of this all, a `TimeStamp` is *unitless*. You should never access its `value`
///       directly
data TimeStamp:
	u64 value;

/// @brief A duration between two time stamps
///
/// @note Unlike a `TimeStamp`, which should be handled as unitless, a `Duration`s value *always*
///       is of unit `TimeUnit.NS`. So, if you need a duration in nanoseconds directly you can
///       access the `.value` field manually
data Duration:
	u64 value;

/// @brief Returns a TimeStamp for the current point in time
///
/// @return `TimeStamp` the current timestamp the system is at
def now() -> TimeStamp;

/// @brief Calculates the duration between two `TimeStamp`s
///
/// @param `from` The time stamp we measure from
/// @param `to` The time stamp we measure to
/// @return `Duration` The duration `from` `to`
///
/// @note The order in which `from` and `to` are passed does not matter because a duration is always
///       the absolute difference between two time stamps
def duration(TimeStamp from, TimeStamp to) -> Duration;

/// @brief Returns a `Duration` from a given time + unit
///
/// @param `time` The time value to get the duration from
/// @param `unit` The unit of the duration to create
/// @return `Duration` A newly created duration from the given time and unit
///
/// @note This function is a useful helper to create new durations. So you could either write
///       ```ft
///       dur := Duration{ 1_000_000 };
///       ```
///       and hope that everyone knows that `Duration` is typed as `NS` or you could write
///       ```ft
///       dur := from(1, TimeUnit.MS);
///       ```
///       and make things easier to understand.
def from(u64 time, TimeUnit unit) -> Duration;


/// @brief Takes a duration and a unit and returns a `f64` floating point value holding that
///        duration of the given unit
///
/// @param `duration` The duration to convert
/// @param `unit` The unit to covert the duration to
/// @return `f64` A floating point value holding the typed duration
///
/// @note This function is used the most when *printing* measured durations
def as_unit(Duration duration, TimeUnit unit) -> f64;


/// @brief Sleeps for a given duration
///
/// @param `duration` The duration to sleep for
def sleep(Duration duration);

/// @brief Sleeps for a given duration, passed as a time + a unit
///
/// @param `time` The time to sleep for
/// @param `unit` The unit of the time to sleep for
///
/// @note This function exists because writing `sleep(from(100, TimeUnit.MS))` is unergonomic so
///       writing `sleep(100, TimeUnit.MS)` is a lot better
/// @note This function does not require a data parameter meanin it results in no allocations when
///       being called. If you sleep for a fixed amount of time in a hot loop, prefer this function
///       over the Duration-based overload.
def sleep(u64 time, TimeUnit unit);
